    topic: "Science / Biology",
    title: "The Remarkable Intelligence of Octopuses and What It Reveals About Consciousness",
    difficulty: "TOEFL iBT+",
    wordCount: 153,
    text: `Among the most cognitively sophisticated creatures, octopuses challenge conventional assumptions about the prerequisites for intelligence. Unlike mammals, octopuses distribute two-thirds of their neurons across eight semi-autonomous arms, each capable of independent decision-making.

This decentralized nervous system enables remarkable flexibility. Octopuses unscrew jar lids, navigate complex mazes, and use coconut shells as shelters—one of the few examples of tool use among invertebrates. They demonstrate episodic-like memory, distinguishing between individual researchers and responding with markedly different behaviors.

Most provocatively, octopuses exhibit play behavior, repeatedly propelling objects through water jets with no apparent foraging motivation. Play, a hallmark of higher mammalian cognition, suggests intrinsic curiosity.

The evolutionary implications are profound. Octopuses and humans diverged approximately 750 million years ago. Sophisticated intelligence emerging along two radically different trajectories suggests consciousness may be a convergent phenomenon that natural selection has independently produced multiple times.

This perspective has catalyzed vigorous debate. If consciousness arises in organisms with fundamentally different architectures, the substrate-independence hypothesis gains support—with contentious implications for artificial intelligence.`,
