    date: "2026-03-16",
    topic: "Business / Startups",
    title: "Why Most Venture-Backed Startups Fail: Lessons from a Decade of Post-Mortems",
    difficulty: "TOEFL iBT+",
    wordCount: 153,
    text: `An exhaustive analysis of over two thousand venture-backed startup failures between 2015 and 2025 reveals patterns challenging the prevailing mythology of Silicon Valley. Contrary to popular narratives, the most common cause of startup mortality is not building the wrong product but mismanaging the trajectory from initial traction to sustainable growth.

Approximately 42% of failed startups cited premature scaling as a primary factor in their demise. Buoyed by early fundraising success, these companies aggressively expanded headcount and marketing before establishing reliable unit economics, and the resulting cash burn outpaced revenue generation.

Co-founder conflicts proved the second most prevalent failure mode at 29%. Divergent visions and inequitable equity distributions created organizational paralysis. Notably, startups with formal co-founder agreements experienced this at less than half the rate.

Market timing accounted for 18%—several promising companies launched technically competent products before the requisite infrastructure or regulatory frameworks had matured.

Intriguingly, second-time founders were not significantly more successful, suggesting survivorship bias inflated the perceived benefits of experience, and the preference for serial founders may be an empirically unfounded heuristic.`,
